---
description: Typed, memoized event handlers — no inline JSX callbacks
globs: "**/*.{tsx,jsx}"
alwaysApply: true
---

# Event handlers

Do not pass inline functions to interactive props (`onChange`, `onClick`,
`onSubmit`, `onCheckedChange`, `onValueChange`, `onKeyDown`, `onBlur`, …).

Declare a named handler, annotate it with the callback type the component
expects, and wrap it in `useCallback`.

```tsx
import {
  useCallback,
  type ChangeEventHandler,
  type MouseEventHandler,
} from "react";

const onTitleChange: ChangeEventHandler<HTMLInputElement, HTMLInputElement> =
  useCallback((event) => {
    setTitle(event.target.value);
  }, []);

<Input value={title} onChange={onTitleChange} />
```

Take the type from the library that declares the prop:

- `Input` `onChange` → `ChangeEventHandler<HTMLInputElement, HTMLInputElement>`
- `Textarea` `onChange` → `ChangeEventHandler<HTMLTextAreaElement, HTMLTextAreaElement>`
- `Button` `onClick` → `MouseEventHandler<HTMLButtonElement>`
- `<form>` `onSubmit` → `SubmitEventHandler<HTMLFormElement>` (not deprecated `FormEventHandler`)
- Custom UI → `TagInputProps["onChange"]`, `AmountSelectorProps["onChange"]`
- Radix wrappers → `NonNullable<React.ComponentProps<typeof Switch>["onCheckedChange"]>`
- react-hook-form submit → `SubmitHandler<Values>`, then a `SubmitEventHandler` that calls `handleSubmit(onSubmit)`

Pass a `useState` setter directly when the signatures already match
(`onCheckedChange={setMinEnabled}`). Do not wrap that.

Leave arrows in `useEffect`, `useMemo`, `startTransition`, render `.map()`,
and `t.rich()` tags. For handlers inside a list, extract a child component or
delegate with `data-*` and `event.currentTarget`.
